<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#volga-trade-route">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volga trade route</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> trade route was a network of river and portage routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the Baltic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and the Islamic world via the Volga River in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a>, slaves and other goods moved south, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> flowed north. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> taxed this commerce, and the Khazars helped spread dirhams into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route.jpg" alt="Volga trade route" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Briangotts · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varangian_routes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The route was established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> who settled in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the early 9th century. About 10 km south of the point where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov River</a> enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> they founded a settlement called Ladoga, in Old Norse Aldeigjuborg. Archaeological evidence suggests Rus trading along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> as early as the end of the 8th century.</p><p>The earliest and richest finds of Arabic coins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> come from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, notably Timerevo in the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>. A hoard at Petergof near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> holds twenty coins bearing graffiti in Arabic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> runic, Greek and Old Norse runic, the last accounting for more than half, and the latest of the coins dates to 804–805.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staraya-ladoga">Aldeigjuborg</a> the Rus travelled up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov</a> to Novgorod, then across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ilmen">Lake Ilmen</a> and along the Lovat River. A portage of about 3 kilometres took their boats to the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Traders carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, honey and slaves through lands held by Finnic and Permian peoples to the Volga Bulgars, and on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a> was a busy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepot</a> on the Caspian shore. From there they crossed the sea to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> trafficked captives taken in raids in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> along the route, selling them to Muslim merchants for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. The Arabic term for such slaves, saqaliba, did not mean only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, since the captives included Finno-Ugric peoples. The silver hoards of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, such as the Spillings and Sundveda hoards, reflect this trade, which functioned from at least 786 until 1009.</p><p>The route lost its importance by the 11th century, owing to the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> output in the Abbasid caliphate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> gained weight. Yngvars saga víðförla describes a Swedish expedition into the Caspian launched about 1041 by Ingvar the Far-Travelled. It was unsuccessful, and Scandinavians made no later attempts to use the route.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Written accounts survive from Muslim writers. About 885–886 ibn Khordadbeh described Rus merchants taking beaver hides, black fox pelts and swords to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the Caspian, paying a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> to the Byzantine ruler and to the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>.</p><p>In 921–922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</a> joined a diplomatic delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to the Volga Bulgars. He described Rus merchants who, on landing, offered food and drink to a wooden stake with a human face and asked it to send them a merchant with many dinars and dirhems. Scholars have disputed whether ibn Khordadbeh's phrase a kind of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqaliba">Saqaliba</a> shows the Rus to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> or Scandinavians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volga trade route” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their economy was closely tied to the <b>Volga trade route</b>, which connected the Islamic world with Northern Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
